\title{zzrctsim: Simulation of Longitudinal Randomized Trials with Conditional and Marginal Data-Generating Mechanisms}
\description{
Simulates longitudinal randomized clinical trials in order to evaluate design and analysis choices. A data-generating mechanism for a continuous Gaussian outcome may be declared either conditionally, through random effects, or marginally, through a covariance matrix or a covariance function of time, with exact conversion between the two parameterizations where one exists and a computable rank certificate where it does not. Visit schedules span run-in, treatment and common-close phases, with staggered accrual and a common close-out date. Missingness is generated from the selection model of Diggle and Kenward (1994) \doi{10.2307/2986113}, calibrated to a target dropout proportion, with mask generation and mask application kept separate so that empirical patterns can be imported; reference-based post-discontinuation trajectories are also provided. Simulations follow Morris, White and Crowther (2019) \doi{10.1002/sim.8086}: 'L'Ecuyer-CMRG' substreams with per-replicate state capture, performance measures reported with Monte Carlo standard errors, and power curves and sample size determination by simulation.
}
\details{
A simulation study in \code{zzrctsim} is five steps, kept separate so
that each maps onto one element of ADEMP (Morris, White and
Crowther 2019) and a manuscript's Methods subsections correspond to
code rather than having to be reconstructed from a script.

\describe{
\item{1. Design}{When are subjects seen? \code{\link[=trial_schedule]{trial_schedule()}}
defines the visit grid across run-in, treatment and
common-close phases; \code{\link[=runin_design]{runin_design()}} expands it over subjects
and builds the model columns. For staggered entry,
\code{\link[=accrue]{accrue()}} draws enrollment times, \code{\link[=close_out]{close_out()}} finds the
common close-out date, and \code{\link[=realize_schedule]{realize_schedule()}} cuts each
subject's grid at that date.}
\item{2. Data-generating mechanism}{What produces the response?
Declare it conditionally through random effects with
\code{\link[=dgm_conditional]{dgm_conditional()}}, or marginally through a covariance with
\code{\link[=dgm_marginal]{dgm_marginal()}} and the \code{\link[=cov_ar1]{cov_ar1()}} / \code{\link[=cov_cs]{cov_cs()}} builders.
\code{\link[=as_marginal]{as_marginal()}} and \code{\link[=as_conditional]{as_conditional()}} convert between the two
where an exact conversion exists; \code{\link[=certify]{certify()}} reports the rank
needed when it does not. \code{\link[=dgm_tte]{dgm_tte()}} and \code{\link[=dgm_mixture]{dgm_mixture()}} cover
time-to-event and latent-subgroup outcomes.}
\item{3. Generation}{Draw one trial with \code{\link[=generate_outcomes]{generate_outcomes()}},
or \code{\link[=generate_tte]{generate_tte()}} for event times. Complete data are always
generated first; missingness is a separate layer, applied with
\code{\link[=dropout_mask]{dropout_mask()}} and \code{\link[=apply_mask]{apply_mask()}}, which is what allows the
cost of dropout to be measured against the complete-data run
and what makes response-dependent (MAR, MNAR) mechanisms
expressible at all. \code{\link[=reference_based]{reference_based()}} supplies
post-discontinuation trajectories.}
\item{4. Analysis}{Fit anything, provided it returns a
\code{\link[=fit_result]{fit_result()}}. \code{\link[=fit_ancova]{fit_ancova()}} is supplied as a reference
method.}
\item{5. Performance}{\code{\link[=run_simulation]{run_simulation()}} drives the replicate
loop against a declared \code{\link[=estimand]{estimand()}};
\code{\link[=compute_performance]{compute_performance()}} summarizes across replicates with a
Monte Carlo standard error on every measure.
\code{\link[=nsim_for_mcse]{nsim_for_mcse()}} chooses \code{B} from a precision requirement,
and \code{\link[=sim_power]{sim_power()}}, \code{\link[=power_curve]{power_curve()}} and \code{\link[=sample_size]{sample_size()}} invert
